**Gross** federal debt includes money the government owes itself, mostly the Social Security and Medicare trust funds. **Debt held by the public** is what is actually out in the market being financed at market rates. Gross debt is 122.6% of GDP; debt held by the public is [98.7%](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/FYGFGDQ188S). The second number is the one that drives interest costs, and it's the one I'll use for the math below. The 1946 comparison on that basis is 106%, and [CBO projects](https://www.cbo.gov/publication/61882) we blow through it and reach 120% of GDP by 2036.

The raw stock, on either measure, undersells the problem. What matters is the flow: the interest bill.

In fiscal 2025, [net interest hit \$970.4 billion against \$916.6 billion of national defense outlays](https://fiscal.treasury.gov/files/reports-statements/combined-statement/cs2025/outlay.pdf), on \$5.24 trillion of total federal revenue. That works out to 18.5 cents of every dollar the federal government collects going straight back out the door as interest.

